tion"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/72229"} "Merlene Ottey"] " (JAM) was back and was favored again, by this time having won the World Championships in both 1993 and 1995. In 1995 she had defeated " [:a {:href "/athletes/75379"} "Irina Privalova"] " (RUS) who had won the 1994 European Championships. But Privalova withdrew in Atlanta after the quarter-finals. America’s best hope seemed to be defending champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/77990"} "Gwen Torrence"] " but she did not make the US team at the Olympic Trials. This seemed to leave it open for Ottey to finally win her gold medal but it was not to be. She led off the curve, but then was outrun by France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/67993"} "Marie-José Pérec"] ", who was considered stronger in the 400, and had already won that event a few days earlier. Ottey held on for the silver medal, her fourth in this event at the Olympics, with Nigeria’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/73968"} "Mary Onyali"] " getting a surprising bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3298"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/67993"} "Marie-José Pérec"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} "22.62 (1 h6)"] [:td {} "22.24 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "22.07 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "22.12 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3478"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/72229"} "Merlene Ottey"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JAM"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JAM.png"}] "JAM"]] [:td {} "22.92 (1 h4)"] [